Why two licences
----------------

They are two different things and they are used by different people. A developer
forking the platform needs a software licence that handles patents and
contribution; an analyst putting a chart in a report needs a data licence that
is recognised by publishers and by open-data catalogues. Apache-2.0 is the wrong
instrument for a dataset and CC BY 4.0 is the wrong instrument for source code.

Attribution
-----------

Suggested form:

    Source: Qeema Open Affordability Index (CC BY 4.0),
    https://github.com/Binary-ly/qeema

If you are republishing figures, please carry the qualifiers that travel with
them — coverage, imputed share, the confidence interval, and whether the figure
is marked comparable. A Qeema number stripped of them says more than the data
supports, and attribution to this project of a claim it did not make is the one
use of the data this project would ask you not to make.

The licence travels with the file: the bulk CSV export sends an
`X-Qeema-License: CC-BY-4.0` response header, because a file passed on loses the
context the API page carried.

Third-party data
----------------

Some inputs carry their own terms, which are not superseded by this licence:

  * Location geometry derived from OpenStreetMap — ODbL 1.0.
  * Exchange rates and prices ingested from an operator-configured public
    source keep whatever licence that source publishes under. The `sources`
    table records the licence per source; a deployment that ingests from a
    source with incompatible terms is responsible for its own compliance.

Operators
---------

This licence covers the data published by this project. If you run your own
deployment, the data it collects is yours to licence, and CC BY 4.0 is what this
project recommends and what the software declares by default. Change the
`license` field on each source in `countries/<code>.yaml` and the
`X-Qeema-License` header if you decide otherwise.
